erification.json"} {"question" "q-81831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/f4d4d52cb24bd1e3dd4bc37736533693ff89e6b08f495f8541f23abde995a5b9.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "f07b529fcc489010236892ec02a2f855802235483748b56921c87bff17bae15d" "basis" 1083 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(5,-1),(2,0),(-2,-1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-1,-5)$\n- $(0,2)$\n- $(-1,-2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "f07b529fcc489010236892ec02a2f855802235483748b56921c87bff17bae15d" "basis" 1083 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-1,-5),(0,-2),(-1,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "f07b529fcc489010236892ec02a2f855802235483748b56921c87bff17bae15d" "basis" 1083 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d5412300abaf3d9cfe40c7fe60724db3bf41b03e257c8e1c9aa4b8a19eb15cd3" "basis" 1083 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "f07b529fcc489010236892ec02a2f855802235483748b56921c87bff17bae15d" "basis" 1083 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "f07b529fcc489010236892ec02a2f855802235483748b56921c87bff17bae15d" "basis" 1083 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "f07b529fcc489010236892ec02a2f855802235483748b56921c87bff17bae15d" "basis" 1083 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "f07b529fcc489010236892ec02a2f855802235483748b56921c87bff17bae15d" "basis" 1083 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "f07b529fcc489010236892ec02a2f855802235483748b56921c87bff17bae15d" "basis" 1083 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "f07b529fcc489010236892ec02a2f855802235483748b56921c87bff17bae15d" "basis" 1083 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "f07b529fcc489010236892ec02a2f855802235483748b56921c87bff17bae15d" "basis" 1083 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-12126" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-2,-3),(-3,2),(2,4).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-3,2)$\n- $(4,-2)$\n- $(-4,2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37dc5109829e0556347c79a4dd02783287eb4376b7bcccfea6a43e6ab4c536b"} {"question" "q-12126" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${270}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-3,2),(2,3),(4,-2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37dc5109829e0556347c79a4dd02783287eb4376b7bcccfea6a43e6ab4c536b"} {"question" "q-12126" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ba09fe4f8b3e8f987b40d1982b4556ee333291d5b16043218e78d30bba6e7ace"} {"question" "q-12126" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37dc5109829e0556347c79a4dd02783287eb4376b7bcccfea6a43e6ab4c536b"} {"question" "q-12126" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37dc5109829e0556347c79a4dd02783287eb4376b7bcccfea6a43e6ab4c536b"} {"question" "q-12126" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37dc5109829e0556347c79a4dd02783287eb4376b7bcccfea6a43e6ab4c536b"} {"question" "q-12130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-1),(1,4),(1,-1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(4,-2)$\n- $(1,-2)$\n- $(1,1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37dc5109829e0556347c79a4dd02783287eb4376b7bcccfea6a43e6ab4c536b"} {"question" "q-12130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,-2),(-4,1),(1,1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37dc5109829e0556347c79a4dd02783287eb4376b7bcccfea6a43e6ab4c536b"} {"question" "q-12130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ba09fe4f8b3e8f987b40d1982b4556ee333291d5b16043218e78d30bba6e7ace"} {"question" "q-12130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37dc5109829e0556347c79a4dd02783287eb4376b7bcccfea6a43e6ab4c536b"} {"question" "q-12130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37dc5109829e0556347c79a4dd02783287eb4376b7bcccfea6a43e6ab4c536b"} {"question" "q-12130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37dc5109829e0556347c79a4dd02783287eb4376b7bcccfea6a43e6ab4c536b"} {"question" "q-81823" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/assets/0c2f0240b962701c2eb2ee34a0eddc164afb8e1a677a5ed7f81fe65efb890358.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37dc5109829e0556347c79a4dd02783287eb4376b7bcccfea6a43e6ab4c536b"} {"question" "q-81823" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/assets/4e0e5c9cee037bded1f5739af35c3a8acfaa4d4e6f469b3e50afe69b96990e39.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/assets/578b3315028bf5a19a4b6e17d228db08b41f24dee63e5cb7f8d601ce667785f4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37dc5109829e0556347c79a4dd02783287eb4376b7bcccfea6a43e6ab4c536b"} {"question" "q-81823" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ba09fe4f8b3e8f987b40d1982b4556ee333291d5b16043218e78d30bba6e7ace"} {"question" "q-81823" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37dc5109829e0556347c79a4dd02783287eb4376b7bcccfea6a43e6ab4c536b"} {"question" "q-81823" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-fie